# Benchmark resolver v2 launch review

**Verdict: conditionally approved for the one existing benchmark cohort**, after the launcher's already-required CPU 11 availability/exclusivity recheck passes. This is prospective only; no benchmark or compiler was run.

Reviewed the resolver-only successor at `benchmark-resolver-v2`. The active pins match: plan `4e03d7f4…`, freeze `c6d894cc…`, launch `3fa1e497…`, resolver joins `1e3508bf…`, and tool snapshot `91bd0c01…`. The 7,031 plan/freeze pins currently match; plan, tool-snapshot, and effects-join hashes cross-bind. Output path is fresh and absent.

The resolver repair is complete and narrowly scoped: the copied base imports 68 relative `./effs/*` files; all 68 are present, with no missing or extra files, and each local file and its installed original matches the joined SHA-256. This includes the formerly missing `effs/print.js`. The candidate wrapper resolves the copied `candidate.mjs`, whose base is selected beside it, so these imports resolve from the private copy without changing installed Bend files.

The original `run.py`, statistics contract (20 pairs, 2 warmups, 10 iterations, Holm-adjusted paired tests, zero allowed slowdown), baseline archive, CPU assignment, and per-command limits remain pinned. The launch still uses the reviewed boundary, adds no runner layer, and writes to a new output directory. The previous 0-pair resolver failure remains the historical result; it earns no cohort credit. Full-process benchmark results, if collected, remain only whole-process API-regression evidence, not hot-path acceptance or compiler adoption.

Validation was read-only: exact hashes, all pinned inputs, effects import/join coverage, installed-to-local byte/hash equivalence, and output absence. No residual blocker beyond the launch-time quiet/exclusive CPU 11 condition.
